<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kikaijima</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kikai Island</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kikaijima is a small island of the Amami group, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amami-oshima">Amami Ōshima</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It resisted the expansion of the Ryukyu Kingdom until King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-toku">Shō Toku</a> sent an expedition in 1466 that brought it under Shuri's control. Archaeological finds show that it was an early link between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and the southern islands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kikaijima.jpg" alt="Kikaijima" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image Science and Analysis Laboratory, NASA-Johnson Space Center. &quot;The Gateway to Astronaut Photography of Earth. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kikaijima.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kikaijima is one of the Satsunan Islands, 56.93 square kilometres in area, about 25 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amami-oshima">Amami Ōshima</a> and about 380 kilometres south of the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>. It is the easternmost island of the Amami chain and, unlike Amami Ōshima and Tokunoshima, fairly flat, rising to 214 metres. A raised coral island with limestone cliffs, it is one of the fastest rising coral islands in the world. It has a humid subtropical climate with frequent typhoons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early Japanese sources name a Kikaijima, though scholars debate whether they mean this island, Satsuma Iōjima or the southern islands as a whole. In 998 the Dazaifu ordered Kikaijima to arrest raiders from the Amami islands, and in 1188 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> sent troops to pacify it. The Gusuku Site Complex, found on the island's plateau in 2006 and in use from the 9th to the 13th centuries, held goods from mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and Kamuiyaki pottery from Tokunoshima, supporting the view that Kikaijima was a trade centre of the southern islands.</p><p>In 1306 the deputy jitō Chikama Tokiie listed Kikaijima among his family's properties, and the islands later seem to have passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a>. A record of 1462 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a> says the island was resisting repeated invasions from Ryūkyū. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a>'s conquest of Ryūkyū in 1609 it came under Satsuma's direct control, and after the Meiji Restoration it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osumi-province">Ōsumi Province</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>. With the other Amami Islands it was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> until 1953.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island forms the town of Kikai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>, and much of it lies in the Amami Guntō Quasi-National Park. Its people live mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, citrus fruit and fishing, with sugar refining and shōchū distilling. The traditional Kikai language, a Ryukyuan language, is endangered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kikaijima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sho Toku sent an expedition against the island of <b>Kikai</b> in 1466, Sho Shin's forces crushed the resistance of Oyake Akahachi in the Yaeyama Islands in 1500, bringing the southern islands under Shuri's control, and Sho Sei campaigned in the Amami Islands in 1537.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
